 The data warehouse then starts syncing your Postgres data. You can see details and progress in the [sources tab](https://app.posthog.com/data-management/sources).
 
+## Selecting columns to sync
+
+By default, PostHog syncs every column of each table you import. To keep columns out of PostHog – for example, to avoid syncing PII – you can choose which columns to sync:
+
+- **During setup:** On the **Select tables to import** step, click **Columns** on a table and check the columns you want.
+- **After setup:** On the source's **Schemas** tab, click **Columns** on a table, or open the table's configuration page and use the **Columns** section.
+
+Primary key columns and the active incremental field are always synced, even if you don't select them, because they're needed to deduplicate and resume syncs. If you select no columns, only those required columns are synced.
+
+Disabled columns are hidden from the table in PostHog, so queries against it won't see them. Previously synced data for a disabled column stays in storage, so re-enabling the column makes it available again after the next sync.
+
+If you add columns to a table that already has synced data and uses incremental, append-only, or CDC sync, PostHog asks whether to sync forward only (older rows keep nulls for the new columns) or run a full resync now.
